WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.350
Welcome to Lecture 5 of the

2
00:00:02.426 --> 00:00:04.780
FrontierCS 2 course, on theory.

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.685
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.756 --> 00:00:09.530
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.162
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:13.930
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.339
The agent passes only if it beats the authors'

8
00:00:17.405 --> 00:00:19.400
code on every hidden workload.

9
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.485
Theory has one task: the binary fuse filter

10
00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:26.710
from a 2022 paper by Graf and Lemire.

11
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.126
We build up to it from a Bloom

12
00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:30.916
filter and an xor filter

13
00:00:30.916 --> 00:00:32.500
then look at the task.

14
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.213
A filter answers one question

15
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:36.920
is this key in the set?

16
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.990
It may say yes for a key

17
00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:40.710
that is not in the set.

18
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.645
That is a false positive

19
00:00:42.645 --> 00:00:45.245
and it happens with a small probability

20
00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.260
called epsilon.

21
00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.330
But it never says no for a stored key.

22
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.487
A filter sits in front of something slow

23
00:00:52.487 --> 00:00:53.376
like a disk

24
00:00:53.376 --> 00:00:56.320
so most lookups of absent keys stop early.

25
00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.807
A filter stores much less than

26
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.540
the keys themselves, but there is a floor.

27
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.214
Any filter with false-positive rate

28
00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.737
epsilon needs at least log two of one

29
00:01:06.804 --> 00:01:08.530
over epsilon bits per key.

30
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.271
For one in two hundred fifty-six

31
00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:12.720
that is eight bits.

32
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.955
The oldest answer is the Bloom filter

33
00:01:15.955 --> 00:01:18.820
from nineteen seventy: an array of bits.

34
00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.230
To add a key

35
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.330
hash it k times and set those k bits.

36
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.898
To look a key up

37
00:01:24.898 --> 00:01:27.120
check that all k bits are set.

38
00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:29.790
A stored key always passes.

39
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.923
An absent key passes only if other keys

40
00:01:32.994 --> 00:01:35.420
happened to set all k of its bits.

41
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.750
Our model stores one thousand

42
00:01:37.818 --> 00:01:39.661
keys in eight bits per key

43
00:01:39.661 --> 00:01:40.890
with five hashes.

44
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.625
Of one hundred thousand absent keys

45
00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:46.142
it accepted two thousand sixty-three

46
00:01:46.142 --> 00:01:47.400
about two percent.

47
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.517
Pause here and predict.

48
00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:51.188
With eight bits per key

49
00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:53.260
would more hashes always help?

50
00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:57.024
No.

51
00:01:57.024 --> 00:01:59.096
More hashes check more bits

52
00:01:59.096 --> 00:02:01.167
but they also set more bits

53
00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:02.810
so the array fills up.

54
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.747
The best k is about zero point six

55
00:02:05.823 --> 00:02:08.411
nine times the bits per key: here

56
00:02:08.411 --> 00:02:09.400
five or six.

57
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.530
Two percent is five times the one

58
00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:14.767
in two hundred fifty-six that eight

59
00:02:14.829 --> 00:02:16.320
bits allow in principle.

60
00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.014
The best Bloom filter needs forty-four

61
00:02:19.076 --> 00:02:20.804
percent more than the floor

62
00:02:20.804 --> 00:02:23.025
eleven point five two bits per key

63
00:02:23.025 --> 00:02:23.950
against eight.

64
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.431
With xor filters

65
00:02:25.431 --> 00:02:28.159
the extra falls to twenty-three percent

66
00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:31.020
and with binary fuse filters, to thirteen.

67
00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.412
They also read exactly three

68
00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:36.330
slots per lookup, not k scattered bits.

69
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:37.870
To sum up.

70
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.589
A filter never misses a stored key

71
00:02:40.589 --> 00:02:43.500
and accepts an absent one at rate epsilon.

72
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:45.574
The floor is log two of one

73
00:02:45.638 --> 00:02:47.298
over epsilon bits per key

74
00:02:47.362 --> 00:02:49.596
and a Bloom filter needs forty-four

75
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.490
percent more.

76
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:53.799
As an exercise, try three hashes and eight

77
00:02:53.868 --> 00:02:55.726
and compare with zero point

78
00:02:55.795 --> 00:02:57.240
six nine times eight.

79
00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.670
Next, the xor filter.
